FINDINGS
1. The floor is 69.6%-75.4% of NPU dispatch on every model in the family (mean 72.8%), across a
   19.7x range in task count and a 16x range in latency. The engine spends roughly seven tenths of
   a dispatch not computing, and that fraction barely moves with scale.
2. During the floor the shim moves 1.33-1.60 GB/s per column, against the 6.899 GB/s per column
   measured as achievable DDR passthrough on this silicon. So the floor runs at 19.3%-23.2% of
   channel rate and 77%-81% of it is not transfer. This generalises to the whole YOLOv8 family the
   18% figure previously measured on SESR M7 alone.
3. Per-task and per-byte cost CANNOT be separated on this family, and the reason is structural: the
   activation packet is fixed at 6,400 B, the median task in every container's audit is exactly
   6,400 B, and bytes per task therefore spans only 8,474-11,870 (1.40x) while scale spans 19.7x.
   Tasks and bytes are collinear by construction. Against the floor, bytes alone fit to 2.6% and
   tasks alone to 12.7%; that ordering is real but it cannot be read as an attribution.
4. Compute is 0.556-0.849 us/task and, unlike the floor, is not a constant - as it should not be.
5. Qwen's independently measured SESR M7 floor, 2.136 us/task, sits above this family's 1.594-1.941
   us/task band, consistent with SESR being the model where the engine loses to AMD's stack.

NOT ESTABLISHED
- No attribution between per-task and per-byte cost; see finding 3. Separating them needs a container
  whose bytes per task differ materially from 6,400, which the current packet geometry cannot build.
- Per-segment fixed cost is not measured here. The inter-segment gap measured separately (20.8 us on
  YOLOv8n, 26.1 us on YOLOv8s) bounds it far below any fitted intercept, which is an artifact.
- No accuracy claim: the NOP copy computes a wrong image by construction, it is a timing control.
- l and x ran 200 iterations rather than 300; every other arm ran 300.
